# wfg-log 1
# fixture fade-stop — a cue faded, stopped, and faded again too late.
#
# The fifth replay fixture, and the one that answers a question the others do
# not raise: what does a log of a FADE look like, given that a fade produces
# fifty values a second and none of them may be written down?
#
# §3.15 is the answer. State transitions are events; continuous readouts are
# not. The GO that started the fade is here, and the tick its run finished on is
# here, and everything between the two is derived — recomputed on replay from
# this log and the document beside it, never read back. A log that carried the
# levels would be a log of the clock, and it would be fifty times longer for
# nothing.
#
# Which is also why a fade needs no audio side at all to reproduce. Hand-written
# and hand-checked like the others; `wfg replay --bundle` re-applies every line
# of it with no Tracktion, no device and no sound.
#
# The graph came up. Four voices, two outputs, thirty-six nodes.
A 0 0 engine audio.editBuilt s:"hosted" i:4 i:2 i:36
#
# The operator armed the standby cue ahead of time, and the audio side found it
# a voice.
A 40 1 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 audio.arm s:"B3N8R5TW" s:"G7W9EF36"
A 42 2 engine audio.armed s:"G7W9EF36" i:0
#
# GO, and the sound started two ticks later. Standby advances to the Fade cue.
A 100 3 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"G7W9EF36"
A 102 4 engine run.started s:"G7W9EF36"
#
# GO ON THE FADE, which is a cue like any other: pressing GO on it is a thing
# that happened, so it gets a run of its own with an identifier of its own. That
# run is what a group will one day wait on (§3.6), and it is the only reason a
# fade appears in a log at all.
#
# Two seconds, sCurve, down to −12 dB. None of which is written here, because
# all of it is in the document — this record says WHO pressed GO on WHAT, and
# the fade is recomputed from the cue.
A 200 5 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"H2K4M6NP"
#
# A hundred ticks later, which is the two seconds, and the fade's run is done.
# The media run is still playing, now at −12 dB — a fade is not a stop.
A 300 6 engine run.ended s:"H2K4M6NP"
#
# GO ON THE STOP, whose verb is `fade`: three seconds down to silence and then
# the clip stops. The media run reads `stopping` from this tick, because a cue on
# its way out says so from the moment it is asked rather than when the sound
# goes.
A 400 7 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"Q6RS8TW2"
#
# Somebody fired a cue that is not in the show. Refused, with the reason, and the
# stop in flight is untouched by it.
R 500 8 cli unknown-id cue.fire s:"NOSUCHID"
#
# A hundred and fifty ticks after the GO: silence reached, the clip stopped, and
# the stop cue's own run finished. TWO RECORDS AND NOT ONE, and the order is the
# point — the fade arrives and reports, and the sound stopping is a separate
# thing observed separately, two ticks later, when the launch handle said so.
A 550 9 engine run.ended s:"Q6RS8TW2"
A 552 10 engine run.ended s:"G7W9EF36"
#
# AND A FADE AT SOMETHING THAT IS NO LONGER THERE. The operator reaches for a
# cue that ended while they were reading the next line. It is applied, not
# refused (§3.8) — fading a cue that finished earlier than expected is what an
# operator does, and there is simply nothing to fade.
#
# Its run is created and finished at once, one tick apart because that is the
# engine's shape: a tick drains a snapshot of its queue, so what a command
# submits is applied on the tick after it.
A 600 11 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"V3XY5Z7B"
A 601 12 engine run.ended s:"V3XY5Z7B"
